  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "jwIrzzFIL0Su4z/nEzQ0m6Mpw9g=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:36:10.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The headline rule from the `inbound-webhook-bridge` recipe:\n\n> **`JSON.parse(body)` \u2192 `JSON.stringify(parsed)` breaks HMAC.** The signature is computed over the exact bytes the provider sent. Re-serializing changes key order, whitespace, and number formatting, so the MAC no longer matches.\n\nAlways verify the raw body string, *then* parse it. This has an architectural consequence: a GraphQL or JSON mutation resolver parses the body **before your code runs**, so raw bytes are already gone by the time you could check them. The recipe uses a dedicated `node:http` listener precisely so it controls the bytes, and recommends the same whenever raw-body access is awkward in your framework.\n\nThe signature scheme is modelled on Stripe. The `x-webhook-signature` header is `t=<unixSeconds>,v1=<hmacHex>`, and the signed payload is `` `${t}.${rawBody}` ``:\n\n```ts\nconst header  = signWebhook(secret, rawBody, Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000));\nconst verdict = verifyWebhook({ secret, rawBody, signatureHeader: header, nowSeconds });\n// { ok: true, timestampSeconds } | { ok: false, reason }\n```\n\nFolding the timestamp **into** the MAC is what makes the replay-window check trustworthy \u2014 it cannot be altered independently of the signature. Verification rejects missing or malformed headers, signature mismatch (wrong secret *or* tampered/re-serialized body), and timestamps outside the tolerance window, default 5 minutes. Comparison uses `crypto.timingSafeEqual`.\n\nThe verified-then-parsed pipeline is ordered: read raw bytes with no parse \u2192 `verifyWebhook()` \u2192 `JSON.parse` the verified bytes \u2192 resolve to a document \u2192 dedup against persisted `processedEventIds` \u2192 map event type to a typed action \u2192 `reactor.execute`.\n\nThe signing secret is server-only and must never be bundled for the browser. The recipe declares it through the package manifest `config` block as a `secret`-typed required entry (`WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET`), the way production packages document required secrets, and loads it from the environment server-side.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`, Inbound Webhook Bridge recipe (\"The headline rule: verify the raw bytes\"), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:36:10.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:36:10.000Z"
    }
  },
